<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cartimandua</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">fl. <a href="/years/43">43</a>–<a href="/years/69">69 CE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century">1st century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Cartimandua was queen of the Brigantes of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> in the 1st century CE and a client ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>. In 51 she handed over the fugitive British leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caratacus">Caratacus</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a>. Her divorce from her consort <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venutius">Venutius</a> split the Brigantes, and when he rose against her in 69 Roman troops rescued her, after which the Romans conquered her kingdom.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cartimandua.jpg" alt="Cartimandua" width="320" height="266" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Francesco Bartolozzi (publisher/printer; printmaker; Italian; British; Male; 1728 - 1815) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cartimandua.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Cartimandua, also written Cartismandua, was a first-century queen of the Brigantes, a Celtic people of what is now northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, and she is known through the writings of the Roman historian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tacitus">Tacitus</a>. Her reign is dated from about 43 to about 69. Although Tacitus first mentions her in 51, her rule may already have been established when the emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/claudius">Claudius</a> began the conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> in 43. </p><p>She may have been one of the eleven kings who surrendered without a fight according to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/claudius">Claudius</a>'s triumphal arch, or she may have come to power after the defeat of a revolt of part of the Brigantes by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/publius-ostorius-scapula">Publius Ostorius Scapula</a> in 48. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tacitus">Tacitus</a> calls her of distinguished birth, so she probably inherited her power. Her name may be a compound of Celtic roots meaning chase and pony.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>In 51 the British resistance leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caratacus">Caratacus</a>, defeated by Ostorius Scapula in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a>, sought sanctuary with her, and she handed him to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> in chains. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tacitus">Tacitus</a> says this brought her wealth and the wanton spirit that success breeds. She later divorced her consort <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venutius">Venutius</a>, who had been loyal to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, and took his armour-bearer Vellocatus as consort, an act Tacitus calls scandalous.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venutius">Venutius</a> made war on her, though she had seized his brother and other relatives as hostages, and then on her Roman protectors, and he built alliances outside the Brigantes. During the governorship of Aulus Didius Gallus, in the years 52 to 57, he invaded. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> sent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cohort">cohorts</a>, and the fighting stayed inconclusive until Caesius Nasica arrived with the legion IX Hispana and defeated the rebels. </p><p>In 69, during the Roman year of four emperors, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venutius">Venutius</a> revolted again with help from others. She asked for troops, but the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> could send only auxiliaries, and she was evacuated, leaving Venutius in control of a kingdom at war with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>After the evacuation in 69 Cartimandua disappears from the sources. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tacitus">Tacitus</a> portrays her notoriously, as a ruler who betrayed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caratacus">Caratacus</a> under the pretence of sanctuary and who put a common military man beside her. His account is the only substantial one, and he wrote of her as a client queen defended by Roman arms. He records that the throne was left to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venutius">Venutius</a> and the war to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Cartimandua” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Caratacus led the Silures and Ordovices in Wales until his defeat in 51, after which <b>Cartimandua</b>, queen of the Brigantes, handed him over to the Romans.</blockquote><a href="/empire/roman-britain">Roman Britain · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Women of high rank could hold power: <b>Cartimandua</b> ruled the Brigantes of northern Britain, and Boudica led the Iceni in revolt.</blockquote><a href="/culture/celts">Celts · Society</a></li><li><blockquote>By the late 40s they were ruled by Queen <b>Cartimandua</b>, who had made a treaty with Rome and governed as a client ruler.</blockquote><a href="/state/brigantes">Brigantes</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in Great Britain, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=53.83,-2.42&amp;year=56" aria-label="Great Britain on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4496 315 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Cartimandua"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4946 585h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4946 585h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=53.83,-2.42&amp;year=56">What was here in 56 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
